<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">League of the Ten Jurisdictions</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1436">1436</a>–<a href="/years/1799">1799</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The League of the Ten Jurisdictions was an alliance of communities in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a>, formed in 1436 after the death of the last Count of Toggenburg. With the League of God's House and the Grey League it made up the Three Leagues, the Free State of the Grisons, an ally of the Swiss Confederation. It lasted until the French invasion of 1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/league-of-the-ten-jurisdictions.jpg" alt="League of the Ten Jurisdictions" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Marco Zanoli (sidonius 15:49, 30 June 2006 (UTC)) · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trois_ligues.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Friedrich VII, the last Count of Toggenburg, died on 30 April 1436 without male heirs or a will. His lands, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prattigau">Prättigau</a> valley, the upper Albula valley with Belfort, Churwalden, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schanfigg">Schanfigg</a> and Maienfeld, faced division among other nobles or inheritance by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>. The communities took a third way, with the support of Friedrich's widow, Elisabeth von Matsch. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davos">Davos</a> on 8 June 1436 leaders of Klosters, Davos, Castels, Schiers, St. Peter, Langwies, Churwalden, Belfort, Maienfeld and Malans swore a pact to protect their rights, make treaties only together and renew the pact every twelve years. No nobles took part.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Within a year eight members had allied with the League of God's House, and the whole league had done so by 1450; in 1471 it allied with the Grey League. The league could not stop the courts and their rights from passing to local nobles. Most went to Wilhelm, Count of Montfort, the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prattigau">Prättigau</a> to the Count of Matsch and Maienfeld and Malans to the Baron of Brandis. Montfort in 1470 and Matsch in 1477 sold their rights to the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, who ruled the eight jurisdictions through a Landvogt at Castels Castle near Luzein, usually a member of the league.</p><p>In 1509 the Baron of Brandis transferred Maienfeld to the Three Leagues, which governed it as a subject territory while it kept its vote in the league. The league signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federal-charter-of-1291">Bundesbrief</a> of 23 September 1524, the constitution of the Three Leagues. Only after buying the rights of high justice from the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> in 1649–1652 could it take a full part in them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The league's first leader was Ulrich Beeli, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/landammann">Landammann</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/davos">Davos</a>, and all its council meetings, the Bundstage, met there. The Three Leagues were a federation of three states rather than a single state, settling nearly all affairs by referendum, and were governed and defended by the communities' joint decisions. Allied with the Old Swiss Confederacy against the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, they were drawn closer to it by the Musso War against the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. After their absorption into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helvetic-republic">Helvetic Republic</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/act-of-mediation">Act of Mediation</a> made them the canton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graubunden">Graubünden</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “League of the Ten Jurisdictions” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
